<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The C7 mark as an Android notification small icon.

  NOTE ON THIS COMMENT: no double hyphen appears anywhere inside it. XML
  forbids one in a comment, and AAPT2 rejects the resource outright, which
  costs a whole build. The house prose style uses a double hyphen for a
  dash; it cannot be used in this file.

  WHY IT LIVES HERE AND NOT IN assets/. Call pushes are sent DATA-ONLY
  (services/account/src/push/fcm-provider.ts: `dataOnly = kind === 'call'`),
  so Android draws nothing from the payload and the default icon app.json
  hands expo-notifications never reaches a call. The three call
  notifications are posted by IncomingCallService itself and must name their
  own drawable, which has to be a resource of this module.

  MONOCHROME ON TRANSPARENCY. Android discards a small icon's colour
  channels and tints the alpha silhouette with the notification's colour, so
  both strokes are solid white and there is no background. The favicon's
  teal and white split does not survive here and must not be attempted.

  STROKED, NOT OUTLINED. A VectorDrawable strokes at draw time exactly as
  SVG does, so the path data below is character for character the favicon's
  (apps/ecosystem-web/public/c7-mark.svg, viewBox 0 0 64 64, the same data
  src/ui/c7.tsx draws). Nothing was converted to filled outlines, so this
  file can be diffed against the favicon by eye.

  THE GROUP IS THE ONLY ARITHMETIC. The favicon's two strokes, with their
  square caps and the 7's miter, occupy 49.010 x 47.080 of the 64 unit
  viewBox (measured by assets/notification-icon.build.mjs, which rasterises
  the same geometry). Scaled by 20/49.010 = 0.408080 and centred, the glyph
  lands in x 2.000..22.000, y 2.394..21.606 of this 24 unit viewport: 83.3%
  by 80.1%, which is the Material system icon keyline of 20 in 24. The old
  PNG's fault was a glyph at 49% of its canvas; this is the correction.
  Group transforms apply scale, then rotate, then translate, and the scale
  applies to strokeWidth too, so 7.5 draws as 3.06 viewport units.

  The 7's miter ratio at this angle is 1.75, inside the default miter limit
  of 4, so the spike renders rather than being cut back to a bevel.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24">
  <group
      android:scaleX="0.408080"
      android:scaleY="0.408080"
      android:translateX="-1.415629"
      android:translateY="-1.058559">
    <path
        android:pathData="M45.5,17.6 A19.8,19.8,0,1,0,45.5,46.4"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="7.5"
        android:strokeLineCap="square" />
    <path
        android:pathData="M34.5,17.6 L52,17.6 L40,50"
        android:strokeColor="#FFFFFFFF"
        android:strokeWidth="7.5"
        android:strokeLineCap="square"
        android:strokeLineJoin="miter" />
  </group>
</vector>
